    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d844">

                <pb facs="0709" n="633" sameAs="frus:frus1916/pg633"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.516/122</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Parker,
                        representing American interests, to the Secretary of
                            State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 336</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mexico City</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-03-20">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 20, 1916</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s instruction No. 1861 of January 20, 1916, with reference to the
                    invalidated issues of paper money in the hands of American citizens. With
                    special reference to the last paragraph of the Department’s instruction I have
                    the honor to state that persistent inquiry among Americans elicited
                    comparatively little information as to the amount of money of these invalidated
                    issues remaining in their hands. Well informed persons variously estimate the
                    amount of this currency in the hands of foreigners at from 100 to 600 million
                    pesos of which probably 25% is <pb facs="0710" n="634" xml:id="pg634"/> in the
                    hands of Americans. In view of the great diversity of opinion a conservative
                    estimate of the amount in the hands of American citizens would be from 25 to 50
                    millions.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles B. Parker</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
